Maestro Resonance must maintain coherence across the flow.
The Maestro must prevent one subsystem from operating in a way that contradicts a higher-priority contract.
If a capability is technically available but violates a privacy constraint, the capability must not simply execute because it is available.
If an output is technically possible but incompatible with the user’s accessibility requirements, the system must adapt the interaction where appropriate.
If a model is available but does not satisfy the required capability, the Router must not select it merely because it exists.
If memory contains relevant information but its use is not authorized, the Maestro must not treat relevance as permission.
If a provider is unavailable, the Maestro must support an appropriate fallback or communicate the limitation.
The Maestro therefore coordinates capability with constraint.
Capability without policy is insufficient.
Context without authorization is insufficient.
Intelligence without continuity is insufficient.
Automation without agency is insufficient.
Personalization without privacy is insufficient.
Accessibility without continuity is insufficient.
Maestro Resonance exists to maintain these relationships.
Maestro Resonance is responsible for preserving state coherence.
The ecosystem may contain multiple states simultaneously.
A system may be listening while a capability is unavailable.
A request may be accepted while execution is pending.
A capability may be executing while another subsystem is unavailable.
A device may be connected while a provider is offline.
A response may be partially complete.
